---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
const path = '/compare/drip-vs-brevo';
const title = "Drip vs Brevo for Shopify: Workflows and Operations";
const desc = "Compare Drip ecommerce events and workflows with Brevo Shopify marketing and application-owned transactional sending.";
const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "When should a Shopify team choose Drip over Brevo?",
    "answer": "Choose Drip when the required Shopify events and maintained workflows fit the retention operator. Validate entry, repeat entry, purchase exits and waiting-message behavior in the actual account; no revenue threshold or universal winner decides fit."
  },
  {
    "question": "What is a positive fit for Brevo?",
    "answer": "Brevo provides transactional email through API and SMTP. It fits an application team that can own recipient selection, templates, retries, duplicate prevention and failure handling. Review the separate Brevo PushOwl Shopify marketing connection for store journeys."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should costs be compared?",
    "answer": "Obtain current quotes for matched contacts, send volumes, required features and peak traffic. Include implementation and operating time, with SMS and carrier charges separately. Attributed revenue alone does not establish incremental payback."
  },
  {
    "question": "How should migration be tested?",
    "answer": "Preserve identifiers, channel permission and suppressions. Rebuild disabled, assign one sender and queue owner per journey, inspect old scheduled messages and test purchase, withdrawal and event replay before enabling the destination. Set timing from verified coverage."
  }
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm"><a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a></nav>
      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="badge">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">Drip vs Brevo for Shopify: Workflows and Operations</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-cyan-100">Compare Drip ecommerce events and workflows with Brevo Shopify marketing and application-owned transactional sending. Choose around the work your team owns, then validate the actual integration and plan with the fixtures below.</p>
      </header>
      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox picks={[
          { label: 'Ecommerce journeys', tool: 'Drip', reason: 'Shopify events and visual workflow entry, goals and branches for a retention owner.' },
          { label: 'Operating fit', tool: "Brevo", reason: "API or SMTP transactional delivery for a team that owns the calling application." },
        ]} />
      </div>
      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
<h2>Ecommerce workflow versus service caller</h2>
<p>Drip is a positive fit for a retention owner maintaining Shopify event-driven journeys. Its Shopify connection documents customer, order and product sync, with specific purchase and checkout event documentation. Its visual workflow gives the team a place to inspect entry, goals and branching. This is a practical operating preference, not proof of deeper data or better conversion than every Brevo configuration.</p>
<p>Brevo is a positive fit when a developer needs transactional delivery through its email API or SMTP alongside marketing work. Its current Shopify help routes merchants through the Brevo PushOwl app. Establish which account, app and data connection will own marketing before equating that Shopify setup with a Brevo API integration. One vendor name does not automatically connect every service or replace a wholesale sales process.</p>
<h2>Candle brand: wholesale and retail identities</h2>
<p>Use a proposed candle brand selling DTC and wholesale. Map the Shopify customer ID, email, retail permission and wholesale status to the actual destination fields. A wholesale buyer may later subscribe as a retail customer: decide whether exclusion applies to a particular campaign or the entire profile. Keep a named sales owner for deals and account follow-up; verify any proposed CRM pipeline independently rather than assuming a Shopify order creates a managed sales opportunity.</p>
<p>Build a welcome branch for popup versus footer signup, with a missing-source fallback and wholesale exclusions. For the proposed skincare shop, use a voluntarily supplied skin-concern preference and a neutral fallback, preserving the purpose promised at capture. If category-view data is used, verify tracking and identity instead of treating all visitors as known subscribers. Ask a second operator to trace each branch.</p>
<h2>Outdoor gear: checkout events and re-entry</h2>
<p>Use proposed carts between $75 and $130 to compare an education message with an optional 10% incentive. Seed wholesale-tagged shoppers, recent purchasers, active winback recipients and open support cases. Test duplicate checkout events, purchase during a delay and changed stock, variant, price or discount eligibility. The intended rule is no competing recovery offer after purchase; test actual dispatch behavior rather than asserting real-time cancellation.</p>
<p>Drip distinguishes Shopify purchase and checkout events; select the event that matches the business moment rather than treating order creation and payment as interchangeable. Its trigger documentation says someone already inside a workflow cannot enter it again; test another cart during that wait and a fresh cart after exit. In the candidate Brevo PushOwl configuration, record the available event and repeat-entry rules and run the same fixtures. Canvas visibility is useful only if the operator understands the resulting path. No merchant A/B result or setup-time advantage is claimed.</p>
<h2>Skincare: transactional API ownership</h2>
<p>Brevo documents API and SMTP transactional sending. For a proposed shipping update, name the application that detects fulfillment, selects the recipient and calls the service. Keep Shopify's existing notification owner until replacement coverage is demonstrated; otherwise both systems may send. Map order ID, shipment ID and message purpose, and separate a necessary service notice from optional cross-sell marketing.</p>
<p>The caller needs a plan for credentials, template versions, missing variables, retryable errors and duplicate requests. Test delayed fulfillment, partial shipments, refunds, changed addresses and a timeout after an accepted request. Keep an application-level send record and inspect delivery/bounce events; an accepted API request is not proof of inbox delivery. Define who investigates failures and how support finds the send record. Do not assume marketing unsubscribe rules alone decide every service message or that an API automatically supplies consent and eligibility checks.</p>
<h2>Cutover and team fit</h2>
<p>Choose Drip when the demonstrated Shopify journeys and their future operator justify the workflow maintenance. Choose Brevo when the demonstrated marketing connection and application-owned transactional delivery meet the team's needs. For the candle brand, retain wholesale workflow coverage; for skincare, retain service notifications; for outdoor gear, preserve suppression and cart rules. No fixed revenue threshold, six-month parallel run or European deliverability guarantee determines fit.</p>
<p>Export identifiers, consent provenance, suppressions, fields and useful engagement history before migration. Rebuild journeys disabled, assign one sender and queue owner for each purpose, inspect obsolete scheduled sends, and test purchase, withdrawal and event replay across the handoff. Keep rollback and failure ownership explicit. Enable on verified coverage rather than a universal migration timeline.</p>
<h2>Cost, permission and operational ownership</h2>
<p>Request current quotes for the same active people, planned sends, peak traffic, support and required automation features. Include implementation, template review, monitoring and export costs. Do not assume a contact count alone fixes either invoice. Keep SMS destination, segment length, MMS and carrier fees separate from email economics.</p>
<p>Hypothetical contribution exercise: a $100 order less $45 product cost and $10 fulfillment leaves $45 before marketing. A $25 discount leaves $20; twenty genuinely additional orders contribute $400 before $300 of incremental messaging and operating cost, leaving $100. A 25% discount does not automatically make an order unprofitable. Attributed recoveries include customers who might buy anyway; use a suitable holdout before treating attributed revenue as incremental lift.</p>
<p>If texts are part of the proposed program, verify the actual sender, eligible countries and account features separately. Preserve channel-specific permission source, wording, timestamp and withdrawal; an email signup or order is not automatic SMS permission. Review applicable requirements for checkout, popup and keyword capture. Assign STOP/HELP and support replies, destination-based quiet hours, frequency limits and failed-send monitoring to a named operator. Test withdrawal while a message is waiting and exclusion of recent purchasers, gift buyers and open support cases. Coordinate email and text offers around customer context rather than a universal twenty-four-hour rule.</p>
<p>Use VIP launch and SKU replenishment messages only where permission and customer/product context support them. Track opt-outs by flow, reply backlog, full-price repeat purchases and cost per additional order. Repeat checks after integration, consent copy, template or workflow changes, with a review cadence matched to sending risk.</p>
<h2>Decision and evidence</h2>
<p>Keep the tool whose demonstrated coverage, operator understanding and total cost suit the required work. Compare useful coverage before adding or replacing vendors. A separate email or SMS product is optional; no mandatory Sequenzy layer follows from this comparison.</p>
<p>Primary documentation checked November 25, 2026: <a class="link" href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/4424695491725-Connect-Your-Shopify-Store">Drip Shopify connection</a>, <a class="link" href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/4424702655501-Workflows-Triggers">workflow triggers</a>, <a class="link" href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/4424710485261-Shopify-Purchase-Events">purchase events</a>, <a class="link" href="https://help.drip.com/hc/en-us/articles/4424702624909-Shopify-Checkout-Events">checkout events</a>, <a class="link" href="https://help.brevo.com/hc/en-us/articles/360014912879-Brevo-PushOwl-app-for-Shopify-Connect-your-Shopify-store-with-Brevo-PushOwl">Brevo PushOwl connection</a> and <a class="link" href="https://developers.brevo.com/docs/send-a-transactional-email">Brevo transactional API</a>. Account behavior and merchant outcomes have not been measured here.</p>
</article>
      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-white">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/brevo-vs-omnisend">Brevo vs Omnisend</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/drip-vs-mailchimp">Drip vs Mailchimp</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/sequenzy-vs-drip">Sequenzy vs Drip</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/home-goods">Email apps for home goods</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/winback">Winback playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>

    </div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Drip vs Brevo FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
